1) Keep the scope ruthless

Ship the smallest slice that proves value; defer edge cases behind guardrails/feature flags.

Define one success metric (e.g., activation or a north-star workflow completion).

2) Simple, boring architecture

Monolith over microservices (one repo, one deployable).

Clear module boundaries; avoid premature abstractions.

3) Clean, consistent code

Enforce a formatter + linter (Prettier/ESLint, Black/ruff, gofmt, etc.).

Small, single-purpose functions; meaningful names; no magic numbers; delete dead code fast.

4) Version control hygiene

Feature branches + small PRs (<300 lines) with descriptive titles.

Require at least one review; block merges on broken CI.

5) Tests that protect the core path

Prioritize high-value tests: smoke + a few happy-path integration tests around the primary user journey.

Use contract tests for external APIs; mock only where slowness/flakiness hurts.

Aim for “tests as safety net,” not exhaustive coverage (30–50% is fine at MVP).

6) CI/CD basics

One fast CI pipeline: install → lint → unit/integration tests → build.

Auto-deploy main to a staging env; production via a tagged release.

7) Observability that fits on one screen

Centralized logs with correlation IDs.

2–3 golden signals: availability of the main route, latency, error rate.

Simple uptime alert to a shared channel; on-call rotation optional but an “owner” is required.

8) Configuration & secrets

Twelve-factor style: env vars, no secrets in code; use a secrets manager.

One config per environment; forbid “works on my machine” settings.

9) Data & migrations

Add migrations from day one; keep them idempotent and reversible.

Seed minimal fixture data for local/dev.

PII: collect only what you need; redact logs.

10) Security essentials

TLS everywhere, secure cookies, CSRF where relevant.

Basic authZ model (role/ownership checks) around the core resource.

Dependency scanning (e.g., GitHub Dependabot/Snyk) enabled.

11) Error handling

Fail fast with clear user-facing messages; return typed errors internally.

Capture exceptions centrally with request context and user id.

12) Minimal docs

A 10-minute README: setup, run, test, deploy, env vars, main endpoints.

ADRs (Architecture Decision Records) for 1–2 major choices max.

Nice-to-have (Weeks 2–4, as usage grows)

Feature flags for risky releases and gradual rollouts.

Background jobs/queues for slow work, with retries + dead-letter.

Rate limiting and basic abuse protection.

Performance budgets (p95 latency target) and a one-pager capacity plan.

Analytics tied to your single success metric (event tracking with a schema).

API contracts (OpenAPI/Swagger) if you expect external integrations.

Basic load testing of the critical path.

Code review checklist (quick)

Does it reduce scope or complexity?

Are errors handled and logged with context?

Tests cover the happy path?

Any security/data gotchas (authZ, injection, PII)?

Is there a rollback plan (flag, revertable migration)?

README/config updated?

Release criteria for an MVP build

Core user journey works end-to-end in staging and prod.

Logs/metrics/alerts in place for that journey.

One rollback lever (feature flag or revertable deploy).

Single owner on-call for the first 72 hours post-launch.

